From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Oyvind Harboe <oyvind.harboe@zylin.com>
Date: Wed, 17 Sep 2026 00:00:00 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] rob: one RobEntryCell module per entry instead of loops over RobSize

Rob's body unrolls its per-entry logic RobSize (352) times: the enqueue
one-hot and connectEnq, the valid state machine, the writeback counting
against every writeback port, the fflags and vxsat accumulation, the
exception flush flag and the trace itype. As one module of 214 k lines
of generated Verilog it is the longest single synthesis partition of the
core: about 40 minutes in abc, single-threaded, with nothing to
partition.

RobEntryCell holds one entry's RobEntryBundle register and every write
to it, in the order RobImp made them; the entry index is an input, not a
parameter, so all 352 instances are one module. Synthesis maps the cell
once and the placer gets 352 copies. RobImp keeps robEntries as the read
view of the cells' registers, and everything that reads it is unchanged.

Behaviour is unchanged. Debug-only fields (perfDebugInfo, debug_*,
basicDebug) are still written by RobImp directly and are not routed
through the cell, so this is for builds with debugEn off.

Carried by the bazel-orfs CoreMark/Joule study; not upstreamed.
---
diff -ruN a/src/main/scala/xiangshan/backend/rob/Rob.scala b/src/main/scala/xiangshan/backend/rob/Rob.scala
--- a/src/main/scala/xiangshan/backend/rob/Rob.scala
+++ b/src/main/scala/xiangshan/backend/rob/Rob.scala
